---
/** Heading in a left rail, prose in the right column. Text-only sections
    otherwise leave half the page empty at desktop widths, which reads as
    unfinished rather than spacious. */
---
<div class="split">
  <div class="split__head"><slot name="head" /></div>
  <div class="split__body"><slot /></div>
</div>
<style>
  /* The body column is sized TO the measure rather than given the remainder.
     It used to be 1fr, which made it 802px wide while the paragraphs inside were
     capped at --measure (~611px) for line length. The text therefore stopped 191px
     short of the right margin, so every prose band ended in a different place from
     the compare table, the gallery and the FAQ, and the page had a ragged right
     edge down its whole length.
     Making the column the measure means the text fills it exactly and lands on the
     grid. The head rail gets the remainder and grows from 330px to ~520px, which
     also stops the section titles shattering across three lines. */
  .split { display: grid; gap: var(--gap-l);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(230px, 1fr) minmax(0, var(--measure)); }
  /* A heading sized for full width shatters in a 300px rail. Step it down and
     tighten the leading; it still sits above item heads. */
  .split__head :global(.sec-head__title) { font-size: var(--step-2); line-height: 1.12; }
  .split__head :global(.sec-head__lede) { font-size: var(--step-0); }
  /* Clears the masthead, which is sticky at top: 0 and 81px tall. This pinned at
     var(--gap-m) — 28px — so on any section long enough to scroll, 53 of the
     heading's 70 pixels sat behind the masthead and what you saw was the bottom
     of a clipped line. */
  .split__head :global(.sec-head) { margin-bottom: 0; position: sticky;
    /* Flush against the masthead, not clearing it by a gap. Any clearance is a
       window between two sticky elements, and the section image scrolls through
       it — a strip of photograph sliding along under the masthead. The masthead
       carries a bottom border, so flush reads as deliberate. */
    top: var(--masthead-h);
    /* Opaque, so the section image passes behind the pinned heading instead of
       through it. The padding replaces the figure's top margin rather than adding
       to it — the resting layout is unchanged, but the gap between heading and
       photograph now belongs to the sticky block, so nothing shows through it on
       the way past. z-index because a later sibling paints over an earlier one
       when neither is positioned, and the figure is the later sibling. */
    background: var(--band-bg, var(--bg));
    padding-bottom: var(--gap-m); z-index: 1; }
  .split__body > :global(* + *) { margin-top: var(--gap-s); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--gap-m); }
    /* Stacked, so nothing scrolls behind anything and the padding that exists to
       cover the section image would just be a second gap on top of the grid's
       own — 56px between a heading and its first line instead of 28. */
    .split__head :global(.sec-head) { position: static; padding-bottom: 0; }
  }
</style>
